# Adversarial review: #1226 tool authorization and residency

## Finding 1 — `call_tool` destroys the inner renderer identity

**Severity: FATAL**

**Failing scenario**

Scenario sequence: (1) The model emits `call_tool({name:"mcp__linear__create_issue",args:{title:"X"}})`. (3) Pi sees the registered outer tool named `call_tool`. (4) `PiChatEventMapper.mapToolCallEnd` copies Pi's emitted name and arguments verbatim. (5) The emitted start event is therefore:

```ts
{
  type: "tool-call",
  seq,
  messageId,
  toolCallId,
  toolName: "call_tool",
  input: {
    name: "mcp__linear__create_issue",
    args: { title: "X" }
  },
  ui: undefined
}
```

Scenario sequence: (1) That shape follows directly from `piChatEvents.ts:240-255`. (3) Execution completion emits only:

```ts
{
  type: "tool-result",
  seq,
  messageId,
  toolCallId,
  output: result ?? {},
  isError,
  errorText,
  ui
}
```

Scenario sequence: (1) That shape follows directly from `piChatEvents.ts:358-387`. (3) The result event has no `toolName`, canonical tool id, child call id, or parent id. (4) The reducer stores `event.toolName` and `event.input` unchanged (`piChatReducer.ts:451-465`). (5) Renderer resolution tries `ui.rendererId`, then `part.toolName` (`bareToolRenderers/renderers.tsx:374-395`). (6) Name-based lookup is therefore `renderers["call_tool"]`. (7) It is never `renderers["mcp__linear__create_issue"]`. (8) The inner renderer receives neither the expected name nor the expected direct input shape. (9) The epic's claim that canonical identity remains separate internally does not help the UI. (10) Internal identity that never enters the event/view model is not preserved identity.

**Concrete fix**

The design must change shape.

Either expose a searched tool as a real Pi tool for the call, or add a first-class invocation event contract carrying at least:

```ts
{
  modelFacingName: "call_tool",
  canonicalToolId: "mcp://linear/create_issue",
  presentationName: "mcp__linear__create_issue",
  rendererId: "linear.issue.create",
  input: { title: "X" },
  parentToolCallId,
  childToolCallId
}
```

The renderer identity and direct inner input must be available on the start event, before a result exists.

Do not plan implementation around the current `PiChatEvent` shape.

## Finding 2 — the inner operation is swallowed, not nested

**Severity: FATAL**

**Failing scenario**

Scenario sequence: (1) Pi adapts each registered `AgentTool` once (`tool-adapter.ts:46-102`). (3) Pi calls the adapted outer `call_tool.execute`. (4) `call_tool.execute` locates an installed `AgentTool` and calls `inner.execute(...)` directly. (5) A normal TypeScript function call does not re-enter Pi's tool loop. (6) Pi emits one `toolcall_end` for `call_tool`. (7) Pi emits one `tool_execution_end` for `call_tool`. (8) No event is emitted for `mcp__linear__create_issue`. (9) No child `toolCallId` exists. (10) No child result can be replayed or reconciled. (11) The UI displays one generic wrapper card. (12) Cancellation targets the outer id only. (13) Progress belongs to the outer id only. (14) Approval state belongs to the outer id only. (15) The inner operation cannot be independently timed or audited.

**Concrete fix**

Create an explicit child-invocation protocol.

The protocol needs parent/child ids, canonical inner identity, validated inner arguments, start/progress/result/error states, and deterministic replay semantics.

Alternatively, dynamically register the selected tool as a native Pi tool and let Pi own the call.

Calling `AgentTool.execute` from another generic tool is not an acceptable substitute.

## Finding 3 — result `rendererId` passthrough does not repair the break

**Severity: SERIOUS**

**Failing scenario**

Scenario sequence: (1) An implementer notices renderer loss. (3) They copy `innerResult.details.ui.rendererId` into the outer result. (4) The result event now has a usable `ui.rendererId` after completion. (5) Before completion, the card still renders as `call_tool` or fallback. (6) On completion, the card can switch renderers abruptly. (7) The specialized renderer still receives `part.toolName === "call_tool"`. (8) It still receives `{name,args}` rather than `{title:"X"}`. (9) Existing renderers registered only by tool name still fail. (10) A wrapper around `innerResult` can move `details.ui` off the extraction path. (11) The UI can therefore flicker, fall back, or misrender successful calls.

`tool-ui.ts:38-50` extracts metadata only from `output.details.ui`.

`bareToolRenderers/renderers.tsx:378-390` proves that `rendererId` is optional precedence, not a remapping of tool identity or input.

**Concrete fix**

Put catalog-owned presentation metadata on the invocation-start event.

Project the inner input/output as the visible tool part.

Keep outer dispatch mechanics as separate provenance, not as the renderer-facing operation.

Add a real-loop test proving the renderer is correct while the call is pending, not only after the result arrives.

## Finding 4 — file changes and progress disappear behind ordinary wrappers

**Severity: SERIOUS**

**Failing scenario**

Scenario sequence: (1) An installed tool edits a file and returns `details.fileChanges`. (3) `call_tool` wraps the inner result as `{innerResult, canonicalId}`. (4) `PiChatEventMapper` looks only at the outer `result.details` (`piChatEvents.ts:365-373`). (5) The nested file changes are not found. (6) No `file-changed` event is emitted. (7) Workbench refresh and artifact state go stale. (8) If `onUpdate` is forwarded, progress is still labeled `call_tool`. (9) If it is not forwarded, progress vanishes entirely. (10) Filesystem inference also examines the outer input, whose path is under `args`. (11) Filesystem attribution can therefore be lost even if change details survive.

**Concrete fix**

Define child event promotion explicitly.

The dispatcher must preserve structured result details byte-for-byte where safe, or translate them through a typed child-event adapter.

Test file change, progress, abort, retryability, and replay through a proxied filesystem tool.

Do not rely on ad hoc object spreading.

## Finding 5 — metering and audit attribution become false

**Severity: SERIOUS**

**Failing scenario**

Scenario sequence: (1) Ten different host tools execute through `call_tool`. (3) `adaptToolForPi` emits telemetry using `tool.name` (`tool-adapter.ts:71-97`). (4) Every completion is recorded as `call_tool`. (5) Per-tool counts collapse into one bucket. (6) Per-connector cost attribution disappears. (7) Risk analytics cannot distinguish a read from a destructive write. (8) Approval evidence cannot prove which implementation ran. (9) Rate limits keyed by tool name apply to the wrapper rather than the capability. (10) An incident review sees a generic dispatcher instead of the affected vendor action.

Model-token usage may still be counted globally, but execution metering and policy attribution are wrong.

**Concrete fix**

Emit a first-class child execution record with:

- canonical tool id;
- connector/provider id;
- implementation/catalog digest;
- grant revision;

